Abstract

The study was concerned with effects of handsfree and handheld mobile phone dialling and conversation in simulated driving. In the main experiment dealing with conversation, 48 participants drove a distance of about 70 km on a route which led through urban and rural environments. In the dialling experiment, the participants drove a distance of 15  km on a rural two-lane road. The experimental design was mixed with phone mode as a between-subjects factor and phone use (yes/no) as a within-subjects factor.

Performance on a peripheral detection task (PDT) while driving was impaired by dialling and conversation for both phone modes, interpreted as an increase in mental workload . Driving performance was impaired by dialling—lateral position deviation increased in a similar way for both phone modes. Conversation had, however, opposite effects—lateral position deviation decreased in a similar way for both phone modes. Driving speed decreased as an effect of dialling with the greatest effect for handsfree phone mode. Conversation also caused reduced speed, but only for handheld phone mode. The effects on speed can be interpreted as a compensatory effort for the increased mental workload. In spite of the compensatory behaviour, mental workload was still markedly increased by phone use.

Subjective effects of dialling and conversation were also analysed. Most participants reported a speed decrease but no effect on lateral position deviation as an effect of dialling or conversation. In the conversation experiment, driving performance was rated better for handsfree than for handheld mode. In the dialling experiment, no difference between the two phone modes appeared.
Introduction

An issue that has been under considerable debate is the importance of telephone mode for the effects of telephoning while driving. Since the physical distraction may be less for handsfree phones than for handheld phones, most international legislation against the use of a mobile phone while driving also concerns handheld phones only. However, both phone modes can cause cognitive distraction which may have negative consequences (Rospa, 2002).

Comparisons have been made between the two phone modes. Burns et al. (2002) compared the effects of mobile phone conversation (handheld or handsfree) in a driving simulator study. According to mental effort ratings by the participants, driving while using a handheld phone was the most difficult. The drivers slowed down when talking on the handheld phone compared to the control condition, interpreted as a compensation for the increased workload. The standard deviation of speed and speed error measures indicated that the drivers had the poorest speed control when using the handheld phone. Reaction times to warning signs at the roadside were slower for drivers using a handheld phone compared to the control condition. The drivers also missed more target signs when they were using a phone compared to the control condition. No effect on lane keeping performance was detected, however. Haigney et al. (2000) also found that conversation with a mobile phone (handheld or handsfree) during driving was associated with reduced speed. The lateral control of the car was impaired with a handheld phone (increased instances of driving off the carriageway) but not with a handsfree phone. Brookhuis et al. (1991) investigated the effects of using a mobile phone while driving on a quiet rural road, a busy ring road and in a town. The phone was either handheld or handsfree. Conversation caused increased mental workload according to self-reported effort ratings. It also reduced the lateral position deviation, which the authors interpreted as an effect of increased alertness. Dialling a telephone number, however, caused increased lateral position deviation with the worst result for handheld phone. Matthews et al. (2003) compared handheld and handsfree phones with respect to subjective workload during conversation while driving on a rural highway. Total subjective workload was higher for handheld than for handsfree phone. No difference appeared for mental demand, whereas physical demand was higher for handheld phone. Strayer et al. (2004) found that conversation via handsfree or handheld phone mode delayed the brake reactions of their participants in simulated driving. No difference was noted between the two phone modes. Using an instrumented car driven on a motorway, Patten et al. (2004) studied the effects of using a mobile phone while driving. Effects of conversation type (simple versus complex) and telephone mode (handheld versus handsfree) were compared to baseline conditions. A peripheral detection task (PDT) was employed to gauge mental workload. The results were that the PDT reaction times increased when conversing, but no benefit of handsfree units over handheld units was found. The content of the conversation was far more important: the more difficult and complex the conversation, the greater the increase of PDT reaction time. It was also found that the driving speed was reduced by using a handheld mobile phone, whereas no speed reduction was apparent for the handsfree mode. Conversation type did not, however, seem to have any marked effects on driving speed.

It can be concluded that where the two modes have been compared, either no difference is apparent or there is a tendency to suggest that the use of a handheld phone would interfere more with the driving task than a handsfree phone. Another importing finding is that drivers tend to slow down during conversation, maybe more so for the handheld mode, a result which could be interpreted as a compensation for the increased workload. Dialling a phone number during driving may have more serious consequences than conversation. These issues need further study, and one of the major aims of the present study is to compare the two modes with respect to effects on measures related to traffic safety. Driving behaviour as well as mental workload are important in this respect.

It is further of interest to compare the two phone modes with regard to subjective effects. If the experience of the users is different from what is objectively true, there may be a need to inform the users about the true effects of using the different modes of phones while driving.

The effects of conversation and the effects of dialling were studied in two separate experiments.
